out hitting attention limits.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nContract-comparison trees compare two contracts (or two versions) for key differences, similarities, and a recommendation. The proven shape: :parallel per-contract structured-extraction (:output-schemas guaranteeing typed provisions), then sequential synthesis :llm consuming the structured vectors (NOT raw text). Always extract first, then diff — feeding both raw contracts to a single synthesis :llm hits attention limits and induces hallucinated provisions.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - compares two contracts (or two versions of the same contract) for key differences and similarities\n  - produces :key-differences (provision-level diff), :similarities (shared provisions), :recommendation (which version to prefer or what to negotiate)\n  - two-input shape: separate :contract-a and :contract-b blackboard keys, not a single :document\n  - natural fit for :parallel extraction across both contracts followed by sequential diff synthesis\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** parallel per-contract extraction reduces wall-time when both contracts can be processed independently (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: both contracts share a roughly comparable structure AND the per-contract extraction is identical work\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:contract-a] :writes [:provisions-a] :output-schemas {:provisions-a [:vector [:map [:topic :string] [:terms :string]]]}}] [:llm {:reads [:contract-b] :writes [:provisions-b] :output-schemas {:provisions-b [:vector [:map [:topic :string] [:terms :string]]]}}]] [:llm {:reads [:provisions-a :provisions-b] :writes [:key-differences :similarities :recommendation]}] [:final {:keys [:key-differences :similarities :recommendation]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** diff-synthesis :llm sees TWO large input contexts simultaneously — hits attention/budget limits sooner than single-doc summarization (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: both contracts are >5KB AND the synthesis :llm receives the full text of both directly\n    - Recommended fix: extract structured per-contract provisions first (compact representations), then feed those CONDENSED structures to the synthesis :llm instead of raw text\n\n  - **Failure mode:** without per-contract structured extraction, the synthesis :llm hallucinates provisions that don't exist in either contract (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the synthesis :llm reads raw :contract-a + :contract-b directly without intermediate structured extraction\n    - Recommended fix: always have an intermediate :llm with :output-schemas that extracts a typed provisions vector PER contract before the diff step — the diff :llm consumes the structured vectors, not raw text\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - contract version-vs-version diff (e.g. provider contract v2.0 vs v3.1 — produces provision-level differences + recommendation on which to prefer)\n  - vendor-contract negotiation review (compare current draft vs prior version)\n  - any two-document structural comparison where provision-level diff matters\n\n#### Alternative #1 — Contract-comparison (confidence: 0.99)\nWhy this fits: Same as the tree-class counterpart; provides the specific 'extract first, then diff' strategy which is critical for legal document accuracy and avoiding hallucinations when comparing versions.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nContract-comparison trees compare two contracts (or two versions) for key differences, similarities, and a recommendation. The proven shape: :parallel per-contract structured-extraction (:output-schemas guaranteeing typed provisions), then sequential synthesis :llm consuming the structured vectors (NOT raw text). Always extract first, then diff — feeding both raw contracts to a single synthesis :llm hits attention limits and induces hallucinated provisions.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - compares two contracts (or two versions of the same contract) for key differences and similarities\n  - produces :key-differences (provision-level diff), :similarities (shared provisions), :recommendation (which version to prefer or what to negotiate)\n  - two-input shape: separate :contract-a and :contract-b blackboard keys, not a single :document\n  - natural fit for :parallel extraction across both contracts followed by sequential diff synthesis\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** parallel per-contract extraction reduces wall-time when both contracts can be processed independently (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: both contracts share a roughly comparable structure AND the per-contract extraction is identical work\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:contract-a] :writes [:provisions-a] :output-schemas {:provisions-a [:vector [:map [:topic :string] [:terms :string]]]}}] [:llm {:reads [:contract-b] :writes [:provisions-b] :output-schemas {:provisions-b [:vector [:map [:topic :string] [:terms :string]]]}}]] [:llm {:reads [:provisions-a :provisions-b] :writes [:key-differences :similarities :recommendation]}] [:final {:keys [:key-differences :similarities :recommendation]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** diff-synthesis :llm sees TWO large input contexts simultaneously — hits attention/budget limits sooner than single-doc summarization (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: both contracts are >5KB