ed. Limit to ~5 sources per synthesis; for more, do hierarchical comparison. Use contract-comparison (parallel-independent task-class) when the structure is exactly diff-two-versions; comparative-summary is for general N-source comparison.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - :sequence of :parallel (or :map-each over sources) per-source :llm extraction → :code aggregator → :llm synthesizer that produces a structured comparison\n  - natural for any 'read N versions/sources and produce a comparison' work — product comparison, contract diff, multi-source fact verification\n  - outputs are typically a structured comparison object (per-source key, common-elements, differences, recommendation)\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** the synthesis stage's :reads include ALL per-source extractions structured as a per-source map — supports apples-to-apples comparison by source rather than free-form prose (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the sources are roughly comparable AND a structured comparison is the value (vs free-form narrative)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:source-a] :writes [:extract-a]}] [:llm {:reads [:source-b] :writes [:extract-b]}]] [:code {:reads [:extract-a :extract-b] :writes [:by-source] :fn (fn [{:keys [extract-a extract-b]}] {:by-source {:a extract-a :b extract-b}})}] [:llm {:reads [:by-source] :writes [:common :differences :recommendation]}] [:final {:keys [:common :differences :recommendation]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** use :parallel (NOT :sequence) for the per-source extraction stages when sources are independent — extraction wall-time becomes max(per-source) not sum(per-source) (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: sources are independent (no need to read source B before extracting source A)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:source-a] :writes [:extract-a]}] [:llm {:reads [:source-b] :writes [:extract-b]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** free-form per-source extraction (different :output-schemas per source or open-ended free text) makes the synthesizer's apples-to-apples comparison hard — comparable fields drift across sources (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: per-source extractions use different schemas OR free-form text\n    - Recommended fix: always use IDENTICAL :output-schemas across per-source extraction stages — comparable fields stay aligned, synthesizer can do direct per-field diff\n\n  - **Failure mode:** synthesizing more than ~5 sources in a single :llm call exceeds context — the comparison loses fidelity on per-source detail (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: source count >5 AND each source's extraction is non-trivial\n    - Recommended fix: for >5 sources, hierarchical comparison: cluster sources into 2-3 groups → comparative-summary within each group → final cross-group comparison\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - product-feature comparison across 3-4 vendor docs: per-vendor extraction → side-by-side feature matrix\n  - contract diff across 2 versions: per-version extraction → key-differences + similarities + recommendation\n  - multi-source fact verification: per-source claim extraction → common claims + contradictions + confidence\n\n#### Alternative #3 — Comparative summary (confidence: 0.85)\nWhy this fits: Similar to its class counterpart, this provides a valid comparative architecture but is generalized for multiple sources rather than optimized for the specific two-version legal diff requested.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nComparative summary is the multi-source research-then-synthesize variant: pa